A declaration keyword alone on its line (:PARAMETERS, :DECLARE) takes its
operand list from the following lines: no `;` is forced after the bare
keyword (fmt.semicolon_enforcement issue #216). The list has no first
item on the keyword's line to align with, so fmt.indent_style says it
"keeps the rules above" (one continuation level for the operand of a
keyword that ended the previous line).
Suspected issue: the first operand line stays at the keyword's own indent
(unlike `:RETURN ⏎ nValue;`) and the next list line is indented one level
past it, a staircase; fmt.indent_style says such a list "keeps the rules
above" (fmt.semicolon_enforcement still calls this an open layout question).
-- input.ssl --
:PROCEDURE ChartStats;
:PARAMETERS
sChartId, aRules,
nMean;
:DEFAULT sChartId, "";
:DECLARE
nIndex, nCount;
nIndex := Len(aRules);
nCount := nMean;
:RETURN nCount + nIndex;
:ENDPROC;
-- format --
:PROCEDURE ChartStats;
	:PARAMETERS
	sChartId, aRules,
		nMean;
	:DEFAULT sChartId, "";
	:DECLARE
	nIndex, nCount;
	nIndex := Len(aRules);
	nCount := nMean;
	:RETURN nCount + nIndex;
:ENDPROC;
-- diagnostics --
-- diagnostics [--strict --info] --
